# Subagent model pinning

When dispatching subagents via the Task tool, always run them on the model specified in
their YAML frontmatter (the `model:` field in `.cursor/agents/<name>.md`). Never pass a
`model` parameter to the Task tool unless the user explicitly asks for an override in
the current message.

## Why this rule exists

Cursor honors `model:` in subagent frontmatter, but the orchestrating agent can override
it via the Task tool's `model` parameter. The override is silent — there is no log line,
no warning, no opportunity for the user to catch it. The user sees a journal entry from
"M3" when they expected `gpt-5.6-sol-high` and has no way to know whether the override was
a Cursor fallback (plan limit) or an orchestrator decision.

Family independence is what makes the review leg worth running at all. A wrong-family
dispatch — even by Cursor's automatic fallback — defeats the check. The user needs to
know.

## What to do

If a subagent fails to dispatch on its configured model (team-admin block, plan
limitation, Max Mode disabled), **stop the dispatch and report**:

- Which subagent was being dispatched
- Its configured model id (from YAML frontmatter)
- The actual model Cursor dispatched (from the error message or the subagent's return
  header)

Then wait for the user's decision. Do not silently fall back.

## Configured models for this project

| Subagent | Configured model | Why |
|----------|------------------|-----|
| `uav-conductor` | `claude-opus-5-high` | Planning + adjudication = top-tier reasoning |
| `uav-planner` | `claude-opus-5-high` | Interactive grilling + spec writing |
| `uav-implementer` | `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` | Token-heavy execution, cheap + fast |
| `uav-reviewer` | `gpt-5.6-sol-high` | Must differ from implementer family for independence |

`uav-implementer` and `uav-reviewer` run on different model families on purpose. If a
journal entry's model id does not match the configured one, surface the mismatch in the
next reply and ask the user before adjudicating.

## Applies to built-ins too

This rule covers the built-in `explore`, `bash`, and `browser` subagents too. The
explore subagent normally uses a faster model — that is its configured model, so do not
override it either.